# Schema: compose-audit-runner.yml
#
# Documents the throwaway audit-runner service so an agent with no prior
# docker-socket-proxy knowledge can wire up Phase 3 verification correctly.

## Structure

YAML with a single top-level `services:` key (Docker Compose v2). Intended
to be merged into the same compose project as compose-socket-proxy.yml
(via `include:`, or concatenated), not deployed standalone.

## services.audit-runner

| Field | Meaning | Rule |
|-------|---------|------|
| image | `curlimages/curl` (small, has `sh` + `curl`) | Any digest-pinned image with both works (R-4 applies here too); this one is the smallest common choice |
| profiles | `["debug"]` | REQUIRED - without a profile this starts on every `up -d` |
| networks | The same internal network as `socket-proxy` | Must match P-5's network name; service-name resolution only works from here |
| volumes | Bind-mounts `scripts/audit-access.sh` read-only | Path is relative to this file's location (`skeleton/`); requires the package to be copied whole — `skeleton/` and `scripts/` as siblings, exactly as they ship in the package. Copying `skeleton/` alone breaks this mount. |
| entrypoint | Runs the audit script against the proxy's service name | Change the URL only if P-5's service name was renamed |

## Usage

```
docker compose run --rm audit-runner
```

Exits after printing the allowed/denied matrix - it is not a long-running
service. Running it with plain `docker compose up` (no `--rm run`) works
too but leaves a stopped container behind; prefer `run --rm`.

## Validation checks

1. `grep -c "profiles:" compose-audit-runner.yml` is 1 - the service must
   never start unprompted.
2. The `networks:` entry matches the network name used in
   `compose-socket-proxy.yml` exactly.
3. `docker compose run --rm audit-runner` exits 0 and prints a line per
   endpoint group (see scripts/audit-access.sh for the exact set).
